  const forceColor = env.FORCE_COLOR;
  if (forceColor !== undefined) { ... }
  const isDumbTerminal = env.TERM === 'dumb';
  ...
}
```

Actually supports-color checks flags, then FORCE_COLOR, then NVIM, then NO_COLOR? Let me not overengineer. I'll do: explicit --no-color flag, then --color flag, then FORCE_COLOR, then NO_COLOR, then CI/TTY.

Hmm, but NO_COLOR is a widely-adopted standard that should win over most. The spec: "Command-line software which adds ANSI color to its output by default should check for a NO_COLOR environment variable that, when present (regardless of its value), prevents the addition of ANSI color." FORCE_COLOR is a user explicit override. Both are user explicit. Most implementations: flag > FORCE_COLOR > NO_COLOR. I'll go with that but keep NO_COLOR before TTY.

Now the flag detection using process.argv includes node's own args but fine.

Then colorize:
